    {
      "@type": [
        "schema:CreativeWork",
        "klawiter:CorrespondenceEntry"
      ],
      "@id": "klawiter:entry/2194",
      "entryType": "correspondence",
      "sourcePageId": 2194,
      "pageNamespace": 0,
      "name": "Format",
      "originalTitle": "http://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Help:Formatting Mediwiki Formatting",
      "sourceTextId": 61685,
      "author": {
        "@id": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q78491",
        "@type": "schema:Person",
        "name": "Stefan Zweig"
      },
      "hasPart": [
        "First line of sub-list",
        "Second line of sub-list",
        "Third line of sub-list",
        "Third line of ordered list",
        "Fourth line of ordered list",
        "Pre-defined indents are:",
        "*indent1 - 3.4em",
        "*indent2 - 6.4em",
        "*indent3 - 9.4em",
        "*indent4 - 12.4em",
        "*indent5 - 15.4em",
        "=Lists=",
        "Creating ordered or unordered lists",
        "Each new line is given the next sequential number (or letter), or in the case of an unordered list, a new bullet.",
        "All lines that are enclosed by the opening and closing tags ( and ) will be members of the list.",
        "Contents",
        "First line of ordered list",
        "Second line of ordered list",
        "Third line of text that is long enough that it will wrap down to a second line but it will not have any effect on the numbering of the list. This is because a new list item requires it to begin on a new line, not wrap to a new line.",
        "Fourth line of ordered list",
        "#first line of embedded numbered list",
        "#second line of embedded numbered list",
        "Fifth line of ordered list",
        "The above code produces the following results:"
      ],
      "bibliographicCitation": "See also: Mediwiki Formatting\n\n=Diacritical characters=\nDiacritics\n\n=Indenting=\nIndenting blocks of text\n\nA block of text can be indented to line up with key positions of the Zweig Wiki text. The text needs to be enclosed in tags as follows:\n \n\nThis is a block of text that will be indented 3.4em from the left margin. It will line up with the text of an entry in a ordered list.\nIn the Sample below the CONTENTS section is indented using this code.\n\nSample\n\nFirst line of ordered list\nSecond line of ordered list\n\nContents\n\nFirst line of sub-list\nSecond line of sub-list\nThird line of sub-list\n\nThird line of ordered list\nFourth line of ordered list\n\nPre-defined indents are: \n*indent1 - 3.4em\n*indent2 - 6.4em\n*indent3 - 9.4em\n*indent4 - 12.4em\n*indent5 - 15.4em\n\n=Lists=\nCreating ordered or unordered lists\n\nEach new line is given the next sequential number (or letter), or in the case of an unordered list, a new bullet.\nAll lines that are enclosed by the opening and closing tags ( and ) will be members of the list.\n\n \nContents\n\nFirst line of ordered list\nSecond line of ordered list\nThird line of text that is long enough that it will wrap down to a second line but it will not have any effect on the numbering of the list. This is because a new list item requires it to begin on a new line, not wrap to a new line.\nFourth line of ordered list\n#first line of embedded numbered list\n#second line of embedded numbered list\nFifth line of ordered list\n\nThe above code produces the following results:\n\nContents\n\nFirst line of ordered list\nSecond line of ordered list\nThird line of text that is long enough that it will wrap down to a second line but it will not have any effect on the numbering of the list. This is because a new list item requires it to begin on a new line, not wrap to a new line.\nFourth line of ordered list\n#first line of embedded numbered list\n#second line of embedded numbered list\nFifth line of ordered list\n\nParameters for the tag\n\n: - specify numbering to begin at 7\n: - specify the type of list to create\n::*numeric (ordered [decimal roman]) - default\n::*ol (ordered [roman / alpha])\n::*ul (unordered [bullet])\n::*bracket (special type for Zweig Wiki, e.g.: [1].)\n\nSubList within the code block\nA second style of numbering withn the code block is achieved using the code. Note the difference in numbering style compared to the embedded list in the sample above.\n\n \nContents\n\nFirst line of ordered list\nSecond line of ordered list\nThird line of text that is long enough that it will wrap down to a second line but it will not have any effect on the numbering of the list. This is because a new list item requires it to begin on a new line, not wrap to a new line.\nFourth line of ordered list\n\nfirst line of the numbered sublist\nsecond line of numbered sublist\n\nFifth line of ordered list\n\nThe above code produces the following results:\n\nContents\n\nFirst line of ordered list\nSecond line of ordered list\nThird line of text that is long enough that it will wrap down to a second line but it will not have any effect on the numbering of the list. This is because a new list item requires it to begin on a new line, not wrap to a new line.\nFourth line of ordered list\n\nfirst line of the numbered sublist\nsecond line of numbered sublist\n\nFifth line of ordered list\n\nIt is possible to specify the starting number of a sublist by modifying the opening tag:\n\nThis will cause the sublist to begin numbering at 23.\n\nSubHeadings within the code block\nThere is a need to have subheadings within a numbered list and not disturb the ordering of the list. This is accomplished by using some web-page codes which affect the presentation, but not the logic of a list.\n\n \nContents\n’’’Text of SubHeading 1’’’\n\nFirst line of numbered list\nSecond line of numbered list\nText of SubHeading 2\nThird line of numbered list\nFourth line of numbered list\n’’’Text of SubHeading 0’’’\nFifth line of numbered list\n\nThe above code will result in the following presentation:\n\nContents\nText of SubHeading 1\n\nFirst line of numbered list\nSecond line of numbered list\nText of SubHeading 2\nThird line of numbered list\nFourth line of numbered list\nText of SubHeading 0\nFifth line of numbered list\n\nNotes:\n\nNotice that the second subheading is not bold. The subheadings are normal text and can be bold or not individually.\nThe SubHeading is created by using the standard browser tag. The tag defines a division or a section in an HTML document. This tag has both an opening and closing tag. Using a DIV allow us to target the text within that division and apply a particular style to it. The style can have a presentation that is completely different from any of the surrounding text. It is applied to a by specifying a class in the code.\nThe \"class(es)\" that are defined as zweigSubHeading(0, 1, or 2) have a 1.75em line height. That is what gives the additional space between the preceding numbered entry and the SubHeading text. They also have various changes in the left margin that is necessary to give the SubHeading the outdent from the number list.\nThe reason there are three different SubHeadings defined is that we need different left margins depending on where we want to the heading to align:\n\nThe first type of subheading is not within a numbered list (it is before the beginning of the list) and would normally align at the left margin along with Contents. Therefore, it needs to be pushed to the right with a modification of the left-margin.\nThe second type of subheading is within a number list. First, to get it to drop down to the next line, a \n (linebreak) code is used. This is not the same as putting a carriage return there because the line-break does not interfere with the list numbering whereas the carriage return would cause the next line to be included in the number sequence. Next, the zweigSubHeading2 class is applied to a DIV. This contains the same 1.75em line-height as zweigSubHeading1 and so gives the extra space of separation. It also has a negative left-margin adjustment to push the SubHeading to the left, giving the outdent.\nzweigSubHeading0 is also used within a list. It is applied in the same way that a zweigSubHeading2 class is applied. It differs from the zweigSubHeading2 only in the left margin setting. It has a negative left-margin setting such that it aligns with the page’s left margin, essentially removing all indenting.",
      "sourceBlobId": 7
    },
