      "decomposedAsWork": "klawiter:work/2330",
      "workTranslation": [
        "English: Stefan and Friderike Zweig: Their Correspondence, 1912-1942. Trandlated and edited by Henry G. Alsberg with the assistance of Erna MacArthur. vi/344p. New York: Hastings House, 1954"
      ],
      "hasPart": [
        "Vorwort [Friderike Maria Zweig-von Winternitz], pp. 5-6"
      ],
      "bibliographicCitation": "[1951]: Alfred Scherz Verlag, Bern\n\nStefan Zweig - Friderike Zweig. Briefwechsel 1912-1942. Compiled and edited with notes and a foreword by Friderike Maria Zweig-von Winternitz. 358p. Illustrated with five photographs: Friedrike Zweig and her two daughters 1912, opposite p. 48; The Zweig house on the Kapuzinerberg in Salzburg and Beethoven's desk in Zweig's office in his house on the Kapuzinerberg, opposite p. 128; Work notices to Zweig's Amerigo, 1940/41, opposite p. 208; Friderike and Stefan Zweig 1935, opposite p. 288\n\nContents:\n\nVorwort [Friderike Maria Zweig-von Winternitz], pp. 5-6\n\nBriefe:\n::[1912], pp. 7-17. Friderike to Stefan, 18 letters, 25 July-19 December. The undated letter of November, pp. 14-15, contains a copy of Friderike's poem \"Sie dienen nicht dem Dichterwort. . .\"\n\n::[1913], pp. 17-52. Friderike to Stefan, 36 letters and two postcards, 5 January-Christmas. Stefan responded to Friderike's letter of 12 March with a copy of his poem \"Ein Paar Verse. . . \" and he sent to her an undated note [March] with greetings from himself, Emile Verhaeren, Romain Rolland, Rainer Maria Rilke and Léon Bazalgette\n\n::[1914], pp. 52-69. Friderike to Stefan, January-30 December\n\n::[1915], pp. 69-82. Friderike to Stefan, 12 letters, 8 March-30 August. 16 September, Suzanne Duchaine [Secretary of the Comité International P.P.P.] to Friderike, 1 letter. Romain Rolland responded to this letter on 31 January 1916\n\n::[1916], pp. 82-84. Stefan to Friderike, 1 letter [undated] and two telegrams, 27 December and one undated\n\n::[1917]. No correspondence\n\n::[1918], pp. 84-99. Stefan to Friderike, 2 dated letters, 8 July and 9 July, 15 undated letters, and 4 postcards, 6 and 8 July, 12 and 13 August\n\n::[1919], pp. 99-126. Stefan to Friderike, 12 dated letters, 29 March-26 October; 11 undated letters; 3 postcards, two dated 20 October and one undated. Friderike to Stefan 5 letters, 6 January-27 October and 2 undated letters. Stefan's mother, Ida Zweig, to Stefan, 1 letter, 23 January. Stefan's mother to Friderike, 1 letter, February. Stefan's father, Moritz, to Friderike, 1 letter, 2 May\n\n::[1920], pp. 127-136. Friderike to Stefan, 6 letters, 30 January-October. Stefan to Friderike, 6 letters, 17 October-25 October\n\n::[1921], pp. 136-150. Stefan to Friderike, 15 letters, undated-27 November. 2 postcards, 21 and 26 November. The letter of 28 July contains greetings from Ida Zweig. The postcard of 21 November also contains greetings from Ernst Weiß. Friderike to Stefan, 3 letters, undated-26 November. Page 151 contains a poem written by Friderike in honor of Stefan's 40th birthday\n\n::[1922], pp. 151-162. Stefan to Friderike, 6 letters, 20 March-undated. 1 postcard, 27 October. Friderike to Stefan, 5 letters, 24 October-31 October. 2 postcards, 22 October, undated\n\n::[1923], pp. 162-174. Stefan to Friderike, 16 letters, undated-5 September. The last letter also contains greetings from Felix Braun, Erwin Rieger, Dr. Max Pirker, Faistauer, Franz Th. Csokor, and Leo Feld. Friderike to Stefan, 5 letters, June\n\n::[1924], pp. 174-181. Stefan to Friderike, 15 Letters, January-undated [July]. 1 postcard, undated. This postcard also contains greetings from Frans and Pauline Masereel. Frans Masereel to Friderike, 1 postcard, 21 July\n\n::[1925], pp. 181-195. Stefan to Friderike, 22 letters, 18 February-16/17 November. 3 postcards, 3 March, 10 June, 18 August. Friderike to Stefan, 1 letter, 4 November\n\n::[1926], pp. 195-207. Stefan to Friderike, 23 letters, 1 February-10 December. Friderike to Stefan, 5 letters, 26 January-10 December\n\n::[1927], pp. 207-219. Stefan to Friderike, 10 letters, 8 February-21 September. 1 postcard, 7 August. This postcard also contains greetings from Erich Ebermayer. Friderike to Stefan, 8 letters, 9 September-undated. Susi [Suse] [Friderike's daughter] to Stefan, 1 letter, undated\n\n::[1928], pp. 219-226. Stefan to Friderike, 19 letters, 25 March-28 December. 1 postcard, undated\n\n::[1929], pp. 226-229. Stefan to Friderike, 6 letters, 1 January-21 March. Friderike to Stefan, 2 letters, 9 May, 15 May\n\n::[1930], pp. 230-246. Stefan to Friderike, 20 letters and 1 telegram, 12 March-27 December. Friderike to Stefan, 8 letters, 7 July-19 October\n\n::[1931], pp. 246-251. Friderike to Stefan, 2 letters, undated [summer 1931], 31 December. Stefan to Friderike, 10 letters, 5 July-18 November\n\n::[1932], pp. 251-265. Friderike to Stefan, 13 letters, 1 January-undated [May?]. Stefan to Friderike, 8 letters, January-6 December. 2 postcards, 4 May [contains greetings from Enrico Rocca] and 5 May [contains greetings from Lavinia Mazzuchetti]\n\n::[1933], pp. 266-274. Stefan to Friderike, 15 letters, 26 January-June. Friderike to Stefan, 3 letters, 23 May, 24 May, 27 May\n\n::[1934]. No correspondence\n\n::[1935], pp. 274-289. Stefan to Friderike, 27 letters, January-December and 1 telegram, 5 October\n\n::[1936], pp. 289-305. Stefan to Friderike, 26 letters, 11 May-30 November. Friderike to Stefan, 2 letters, 1 July, 29 September\n\n::[1937], pp. 305-320. Stefan to Friderike, 21 letters, 28 January-17 July. 1 postcard, 5 February [contains greetings from Gussy Jannings and Emil (?)]\n\n::[1938]. No correspondence\n\n::[1939]. No correspondence\n\n::[1940], pp. 320-332. Stefan to Friderike, 15 letters, undated-31 December and 1 undated telegram. The 7 letters and the 1 telegram on pp. 320-324 were written in French\n\n::[1941], pp. 332-351. Stefan to Friderike, 24 letters, 23 January-28 December. The letter of 15 December was written in English and Friderike translated it into German. Both versions are printed here, pp. 348-350\n\n::[1942], pp. 351-358. Stefan to Friderike, 4 letters, 20 January-22 February. Stefan wrote these letters in English and Friderike Maria translated them into German. Both versions are printed here\n\n[1981]: Alfred Scherz Verlag, Bern\n\nStefan Zweig - Friderike Zweig. Unrast der Liebe. Ihr Leben und ihre Zeit im Spiegel ihres Briefwechsels. Revised edition. Compiled and edited with an appendix by Petra Eisele. 351/(1)p. 2nd impression 1982. This impression was also printed as a licensed edition for the NSB Buch & Phono-Club, Zürich, 1982. See p. 290, Stefan to Friderike, 1 letter, 18 Feb. 1942 [Facsimile]\n\n[2006]: S. Fischer Verlag, Frankfurt am Main\n\nStefan Zweig - Friderike Zweig. \"Wenn einen Augenblick die Wolken weichen\". Briefwechsel 1912-1942. Second revised edition. Edited by Jeffrey B. Berlin and Gert Kerschbaumer. 433/(14)p. \n\nTranslations:\n\nEnglish: Stefan and Friderike Zweig: Their Correspondence, 1912-1942. Trandlated and edited by Henry G. Alsberg with the assistance of Erna MacArthur. vi/344p. New York: Hastings House, 1954\n\nFrench: L’Amour inquiet, Friderike et Stefan Zweig. Correspondance 1912-1942. Translated by Jacques Legrand. 497p. Paris: Editions Des Femmes, 1988\n\nSpanish: Correspondencia: Stefan Zweig / Friderike Zweig. Translated by Fernando Trias. 401p. Barcelona: AHR, 1957\n\nTurkish: Friderike’ye mektuplar. Excerpts from the letters. Translated by Burhan Arpad. 87p. Istanbul: Yaylacik Matbaasi, 1967 [Yanki Yayinlari, Seri 9] 358/(2)p. Bern: Alfred Scherz Verlag, 1951",
      "sourceBlobId": 2
    },
