      "@id": "klawiter:entry/3782",
      "entryType": "film",
      "sourcePageId": 3782,
      "pageNamespace": 0,
      "name": "Beware of Pity / Film and Stage Play",
      "sourceTextId": 45244,
      "author": {
        "@id": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q78491",
        "@type": "schema:Person",
        "name": "Stefan Zweig"
      },
      "datePublished": "1946",
      "allYears": [
        1946,
        2015,
        2017,
        2019
      ],
      "timePeriod": "post-wwii",
      "publisher": {
        "@id": "klawiter:publisher/Two%20Cities%20Films",
        "@type": "schema:Organization",
        "name": "Two Cities Films"
      },
      "locationCreated": {
        "@id": "klawiter:place/London",
        "@type": "schema:Place",
        "name": "London",
        "sameAs": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q84"
      },
      "allLocations": [
        "London",
        "Rome",
        "Berlin",
        "Sydney",
        "Melbourne"
      ],
      "numberOfPages": 12,
      "categories": [
        "Films / Plays / Operas"
      ],
      "mainCategory": "Films",
      "decomposedAsWork": "klawiter:work/3782",
      "bibliographicCitation": "[1946]: Two Cities Films, London and Rome\n\nBeware of Pity [A black and white film drama based on Stefan Zweig's novel \"Ungeduld des Herzens\"]\n\nLanguage: English\nDirector: Maurice Elvey\nProducer: Maurice Elvey and W. P. Lipscomb\nScript writers: Elizabeth Baron, W. P. Lipscomb, and Marguerite Stern\nCast: Lilli Palmer, Albert Lieven, Cedric Hardwicke, Gladys Cooper et al.\nRuntime: 1 hour and 45 minutes\nReleased in England on 22 July 1946\n\n[2017]: Barbican, London\n\nBeware of Pity [A stage play based on Stefan Zweig's novel \"Ungeduld des Herzens\"]. Staged by Simon McBurney at the Barbican, 9-12 February. Frist staged By Simon McBurney at the Schaubühne , Berlin, December 2015\n\nDiscussion:. Simon McBurney. \"Beware of Pity is chillingly resonant in the Trump era\" in The Guardian [London], 3 February 2017. See: http://www/theguardian.com/.../simon-mcburney-beware-of-pity-Stefan-Zweig-trump\n\nReviews:\n\nNice, David. \"Beware of Pity, Complicite & Schaubühne, Berlin, Barbican\" in The Arts Desk [Cockfosters, London], 10 February 2017. Contains 2 photographs from scenes of the play. See: http://www.theartsdesk.com/theatre/beware-pity-complicite-schaub%C3%BChne-berlin-barbican\n\nStand, John. \"Beware of Pity review: Go and share a dream of pity and of love\" in The Sydney Morning Herald [Sydney, Australia], 24 January 2019. A review of Simon McBurney's play Beware of Pity based upon Stefan Zweig's novel Ungeduld des Herzens . The play was presented on stage in the Roslyn Packer Theatre, Walsh Bay (Sydney), from 23 to 27 January 2019. Christoph Gawenda and Laurenz Laufenberg play Hofmiller; Marie Burchard plays Edith; Eva Meckbach plays both Edith and Illona. Music and sound by Pete Malkin; video by Will Duke; the set by Anna Fleischle; and the costumes by Holly Waddington. See: https://www.smh.com.au/entertainment/theatre/beware-of-pity-review-go-and-share-a-dream-of-pity-and-of-love-20190124-h1af2l.html\n\nShand, John. \"Simon McBurney: collaborating across the language divide\" in The Age [Melbourne, Australia], 15 January 2019. Concerning Ungeduld des Herzens, a German stage play based on Beware of Pity in a version by Simon McBurney, James Yeatman, Maja Zade and the ensemble. Presented on stage in the Schaubühne am Lehniner Platz, Berlin in January 2019. See: https://www.theage.com.au/entertainment/theatre/simon-mcburney-collaborating-across-the-language-divide-20190110-h19xcv.html\n\nTrueman, Matt. \"Stefan Zweig: Complicite's Simon McBurney on why he matters\" in The Financial Times [London], 28 January 2017, Life and Arts, p. 12. Photograph by Gianmarco Bresadola. See: Google: Matt Trueman. Stefan Zweig: Complicite's Simon McBurney on why he matters\n\nWake, Caroline. \"Beware of Pity is beautiful to watch, but clashes with modern sensibilities\" in The Conversation [Website, Melbourne, Australia], 24 January 2019. See: http://theconversation.com/beware-of-pity-is-beautiful-to-watch-but-clashes-with-modern-sensibilities-110440",
      "sourceBlobId": 4
    },
