      "name": {
        "@value": "Running 'Amok' at DPAC",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "sourceTextId": 59104,
      "datePublished": "2018",
      "timePeriod": "contemporary",
      "publisher": {
        "@id": "klawiter:publisher/The%20Edge%20Malaysia",
        "@type": "schema:Organization",
        "name": "The Edge Malaysia"
      },
      "locationCreated": {
        "@id": "klawiter:place/Kuala%20Lampur",
        "@type": "schema:Place",
        "name": "Kuala Lampur"
      },
      "inLanguage": "en",
      "languageName": "English",
      "categories": [
        "Secondary Literature / Titles (English)",
        "Films / Plays / Operas"
      ],
      "mainCategory": "Secondary Literature",
      "bibliographicCitation": "\"Running 'Amok' at DPAC\" [Damansara Performing Arts Centre, Kuala Lampur. Eastern and Western Influences meet in an Austrian novella adapted by a Frenchman (Alexis Moncorgé) for Malaysian theatre]. Alexis Moncorgé's theatrical adaption of \"Schachnovelle\" was staged on May 4 and 5, 2018 at the Damansara Performing Arts Centre. This article first appeared on April 30, 2018 in The Edge Malaysia [Kuala Lampur]\n\nOnline article: http://www.optionstheedge.com/topic/culture/running-amok-dpac",
      "sourceBlobId": 6
    },
    {
      "@type": [
        "schema:Article",
        "klawiter:EssayEntry"
      ],
      "@id": "klawiter:entry/5366",
      "entryType": "essay",
      "sourcePageId": 5366,
      "pageNamespace": 0,
      "name": {
        "@value": "Whither Jewry?",
        "@language": "de"
      },
      "sourceTextId": 52346,
      "author": {
        "@id": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q78491",
        "@type": "schema:Person",
        "name": "Stefan Zweig"
      },
      "datePublished": "2019",
      "allYears": [
        1935,
        1936,
        1958,
        2019,
        2020
      ],
      "timePeriod": "contemporary",
      "locationCreated": {
        "@id": "klawiter:place/Wien",
        "@type": "schema:Place",
        "name": "Wien",
        "sameAs": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1741"
      },
      "allLocations": [
        "London",
        "Salzburg",
        "Wien",
        "Paris"
      ],
      "inLanguage": "de",
      "languageName": "German",
      "numberOfPages": 262,
      "categories": [
        "Essays / Individual Essays (German)"
      ],
      "mainCategory": "Essays",
      "bibliographicCitation": "\"Die Frage 'Whither Jewry' ist schwer zu beantworten...\". The original manuscript contains 4 leaves and is obviously a fragment. It used to be in the Stefan Zweig Archives in London; now it is located in the archives of the Stefan Zweig Zentrum Salzburg. The German original is printed in \"Worte haben keine Macht mehr\" [Wien, 2019], No. 26, pp. 152-154. Commentaries, p. 262. \n\nEnglish translation:\n\n\"Whither Jewry?\" is an address prepared for a symposium sponsored by the Yiddish periodical Dos Yiddishe Vort in London in 1935. This address was translated into English by Joseph Leftwish, who also read it for Stefan Zweig at the symposium\n\nA fragment of the Joseph Leftwich translation is included on pp. 93-94 in his article \"Stefan Zweig and the World of Yesterday\" in Year Book [Leo Baeck Institute, London], III [1958], pp. 81-100\n\nSee also: \"What will happen to the Jews? [A letter by Stefan Zweig to Joseph Leftwich, 23 April 1936] in \"Worte haben keine Macht mehr\" [Wien, 2019], No. 28, pp. 161-163. German version: \"Was wird mit Juden geschehen?\", No. 47, pp. 239-241\n\nFrench translation: \"Où va le judaïsme?\" in Stefan Zweig. L'Esprit européen en exil [Paris, 2020], No. 12, pp. (121)-125. Translated into French by Jacques Le Rider",
      "sourceBlobId": 6
    },
